.TH P910ND 8 "16 January 2014"
.SH NAME
p910nd \- port 9100+n printer daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p910nd
[\fB-f device\fR]
[\fB-i bindaddr\fR]
[\fB-bvd\fR]
[\fB0|1|2\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I p910nd
is a small daemon that copies any data received on the port
it is listening on to the corresponding printer port.
It is primarily intended for diskless Linux hosts running as printer drivers
but there is no reason why it could not be used on diskful hosts.
Port 9100 is copied to /dev/lp0, 9101 to /dev/lp1 and 9102 to /dev/lp2.
The default is port 9100 to /dev/lp0.
.LP
The \fB-f\fR option can be used to specify a different printer device,
e.g. /dev/usblp0.
.LP
The \fB-i\fR option can be used to specify binding to one address instead
of all interfaces which is the default.
.LP
The \fB-b\fR option turns on bidirectional copying.  After the client half-closes,
the connection is held only long enough for the printer's reply (status query
answer) to drain; once both buffers are empty the job ends as soon as there has
been no printer-to-network progress for \fBPRINTER_REPLY_WINDOW\fR seconds
(default 60, change with \fB-DPRINTER_REPLY_WINDOW=n\fR).  The window is measured
from the last real reply movement, so a still-talking device is never cut off.
If the printer has not answered a status query at all, the connection is held for
the full window (default 60s) so a printer that is out of paper or off line can
still report the fault after tens of seconds or minutes; once it has started to
reply, the job closes \fBPRINTER_REPLY_IDLE\fR seconds (default 5) after the last
activity, keeping a normal bi-directional job quick.
.LP
The \fB-v\fR option shows the version number and exits.
.LP
The \fB-d\fR option causes the daemon to run in the foreground in
standalone mode and prints log messages to stdout for debugging.
Under \fB(x)inetd\fR, where the accepted socket occupies the standard input,
output and error descriptors, log messages go to \fBsyslog\fR(3) instead:
writing them to stdout would send them to the client as part of the print job.
\fB-d\fR never changes whether the process is a daemon or a one-shot service;
that is decided solely by whether it was handed a socket.
.LP
A connection on which no data has moved in either direction for
30 seconds (change with \fB-DIDLE_TIMEOUT=n\fR at build time) is closed,
so a client that connects and never sends anything cannot block the
daemon.  A connection that has already streamed data is abandoned only after
\fBSILENT_TIMEOUT\fR seconds (default 120, change with \fB-DSILENT_TIMEOUT=n\fR) of
no progress, so a legitimate mid-job pause (a slow client, a large job sent in
pieces, a client that sends a header then waits) is not mistaken for a dead client
and truncated.  A printer that accepts no data is given a recoverable
\fBPRINTER_STALL_TIMEOUT\fR (default 120s) to recover from being out of paper or
off line before the job is abandoned and reported as failed.
Every client is served: there is no limit on the number of concurrent jobs.
Jobs for one printer are serialised by a lock and simply queue for their turn,
so a busy printer delays a job instead of rejecting it.  The printer device is
retried when it cannot be opened yet (\fBOPEN_PRINTER_RETRY_INTERVAL\fR seconds
between attempts, default 10, change with
\fB-DOPEN_PRINTER_RETRY_INTERVAL=n\fR): a temporary failure (device busy,
device node present but its backend not ready, interrupted, out of resources)
is retried for as long as it takes, so the printer being still switched off
when the service starts does not lose the job.  A permanent failure (device
node missing, backend detached, permission denied, I/O error) is abandoned
once \fBOPEN_PRINTER_PERMANENT_WAIT\fR seconds (default 30, change with
\fB-DOPEN_PRINTER_PERMANENT_WAIT=n\fR) have passed since the client connected,
giving the user that long to switch the printer on while a job whose printer
is simply absent does not hold the queue forever; the abandoned job is
reported to the client as a failure.  SIGTERM stops either wait.
Descriptors of any number are supported (the I/O loop uses \fBpoll\fR(2), not
\fBselect\fR(2)), so a long-running daemon never refuses a connection for its
descriptor number.
.LP
Jobs for one printer are serialised by a byte range of the lock file, and a
busy printer makes a job queue rather than refuse it.  That wait has no time
limit by default: losing a print job is worse than making the next one wait.
\fBLOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT\fR (default 0, i.e. wait forever; change with
\fB-DLOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT=n\fR) bounds it.  When the bound expires the job is
abandoned and the client is told the job failed, so it retries instead of
hanging; pick a value comfortably longer than the slowest legitimate job.
.LP
Every timeout is measured with \fBclock_gettime\fR(2) and
\fBCLOCK_MONOTONIC\fR where the platform provides it, so changing the system
clock cannot make a job hang or abort.  Where it is unavailable the daemon
falls back to \fBgettimeofday\fR(3); define \fB-DNO_CLOCK_GETTIME=1\fR to force
that fallback.  On glibc older than 2.17 \fBclock_gettime\fR(2) lives in
\fIlibrt\fR, so add \fB-lrt\fR to \fBLIBS\fR there.
.LP
The lock file is created with mode 0644 in a directory that the daemon creates
if it is missing.  Point \fBLOCKFILE_DIR\fR somewhere else when
\fI/var/lock/subsys\fR does not exist or is not writable; the directory is
never unlinked, so a stale file is harmless.
.SH INSTALLATION
.I p910nd
can be run as a standalone daemon or from (x)inetd.
It will automatically detect if it is running under (x)inetd.
.LP
A sample SysVinit script,
.IR p910nd.init ,
is installed as
.IR /etc/init.d/p910nd .
It stops the daemon through the pid file the daemon writes, because the
daemon renames its own process to
.I p9100d
so that
.BR ps (1)
shows the printer port, and the name-based lookup would no longer match.
.I p910nd
will change its name under ps to match the printer port, i.e.
.I p9100d, p9101d
and
.IR p9102d .
.LP
When running under (x)inetd, the
.I /etc/inetd.conf
entry should look something like this (with tcpwrappers protection):
.sp
.nf
p9101 stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/tcpd /sbin/p910nd
.fi
.sp
Don't forget to add an entry in
.I /etc/services
for the corresponding port.
.LP
If operating with lprng, use the host%port syntax for the
printer device to send jobs to it.
.LP
If operating with CUPS, this is supported as the AppSocket
protocol, also known as the JetDirect (probably TM) protocol.
.LP
With classic Berkeley lpd(8) no helper program is needed: lpd(8) prints with
pr(1), and
.sp
.nf
lp|\fBd\fB:\fP\fIhost\fP\fB:\fP9100\fB\fR
.fi
.sp
sends the job to port 9100 of \fIhost\fR, which is what this daemon serves.
An /etc/printcap entry therefore only needs
.sp
.nf
lp=:\fBpr\fR=\fI/usr/bin/lp\fB:\fP9100\fB\fR
.fi
.sp
on the machine running this daemon, plus a dummy spool file where lpd(8)
insists on one; that file is opened but never written to.
.LP
When running under inetd, more than one instance could be started.
To avoid problems with multiple instances attempting to access the
printer at the same time, make sure that only one client is active
at any one time. This can be done by designating one host as the
spooler and sending all jobs to this host. You will probably
need to set up an intermediate queue anyway to provide print job filtering.
.LP
If built with USE_WRAP, it uses the libwrap library (tcpwrappers).
Access control can be done with /etc/hosts.allow
and /etc/hosts.deny. The service name is p910nd.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I p910nd
logs error messages to syslog.
Error messages use
.IR strerror (3),
they never contain the glibc specific \fB%m\fR conversion.
.SH SIGNALS
.I p910nd
ignores SIGPIPE, so a client that disappears does not kill the daemon.
On SIGTERM and SIGINT it removes its pid file and releases the printer
lock before exiting.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
printcap(5), hosts_access(5)
.SH FILES
.TP
/var/run/p9100d.pid
Written when the daemon forks into the background, removed when it exits
normally or on SIGTERM/SIGINT.  Not written with \fB-d\fR.
.TP
/var/lock/subsys/p9100d
The lock file: byte 0 is the instance lock, byte 1 the per-job lock.  The
directory is created if missing and the file is never unlinked, so a stale one
is harmless.  Override the directory with \fB-DLOCKFILE_DIR=\"...\"\fR.
.TP
/etc/hosts.allow, /etc/hosts.deny
Used only when built with \fBUSE_WRAP\fR.
.SH "TUNING AT BUILD TIME"
Every one of these is a \fB-D\fR macro; the default is what a normal
installation wants.
.TP
.B IDLE_TIMEOUT
(30) seconds a connection may carry no data in either direction before it is
closed, counted only while both buffers are empty.
.TP
.B SILENT_TIMEOUT
(120) seconds of no progress after a client has actually streamed data.  A
mid-job pause is not a dead client.
.TP
.B PRINTER_STALL_TIMEOUT
(120) seconds a printer may accept none of the pending data before the job is
abandoned.  0 waits forever.
.TP
.B PRINTER_FLUSH_TIMEOUT
(10) seconds allowed for the last buffered bytes to reach the printer after
the job loop ended.
.TP
.B PRINTER_DRAIN_TIMEOUT
(5) seconds to wait for the parallel port to report idle before the device is
closed.
.TP
.B PRINTER_REPLY_WINDOW
(60) seconds the client is held after it half-closed, waiting for a status
reply that never arrived.
.TP
.B PRINTER_REPLY_IDLE
(5) seconds of silence after the printer did reply before the connection is
released.
.TP
.B PRINTER_EOF_ZERO_READS
(20) consecutive zero-byte reads from the printer that mean end of data.  Only
consulted once the print direction is finished.
.TP
.B PRINTER_READ_PACE_US
(100000) microseconds to wait between two reads from the printer in
bidirectional mode, so a chatty device cannot dominate.
.TP
.B NO_PROGRESS_USLEEP
(20000) microseconds to pause after an iteration that moved nothing.
.TP
.B DRAIN_TIMEOUT
(10) seconds of draining a socket before it is closed.
.TP
.B DRAIN_MAX_BYTES
(1 MiB) of unread data to drain before the connection is cut.
.TP
.B SOCKET_RCVTIMEO, SOCKET_SNDTIMEO
(5) seconds socket timeouts, independent of IDLE_TIMEOUT.
.TP
.B NETWORK_STALL_TIMEOUT
(120) seconds the printer-to-network direction may make no progress before
the job is ended.
.TP
.B OPEN_PRINTER_RETRY_INTERVAL
(10) seconds between attempts to open a printer that is not ready.
.TP
.B OPEN_PRINTER_PERMANENT_WAIT
(30) seconds a permanently failing printer is tolerated, counted from the
moment the client connected.
.TP
.B LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT
(0 = forever) seconds a job waits for the printer lock.
.TP
.B SHUTDOWN_GRACE
(30) seconds in-flight jobs get to finish on SIGTERM before they are signalled
and then killed.
.TP
.B FAIL_WITH_RST
(1) close a failed job with RST so the client knows to retry.  0 restores the
older clean-FIN behaviour.
.TP
.B NO_CLOCK_GETTIME
unset; define it to measure every timeout with gettimeofday(3) instead of
CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
.TP
.B PRINTERFILE
(\"/dev/lp%c\") the device template the printer number is substituted into.
Set it to point somewhere else when there is no lp device, e.g.
\fB-DPRINTERFILE='\"/dev/usblp%c\"'\fR.  The \fB-f\fR option overrides it at
run time.
